  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A distant Nor'Easter brings hazardous marine \nconditions, with gusty northeast breezes over land through\nThursday. Other than some light rain showers on Wednesday in \nsoutheast New England, dry weather otherwise prevails. \n\nNor'Easter located well offshore (well south of 40N/70W) will \ncontinue to slowly meander nearly in place through most of \nWednesday, then gradually peel off toward the NE and ENE later on \nWednesday and into the waters south of the Canada Maritimes \nThursday. Meanwhile, an expansive area of high pressure continues to \ngovern much of New England with sunny skies, but is driving a tight \nNE gradient bringing breezy to gusty conditions, with the strongest \ngusts (35-40 mph) closer to Cape Cod and Nantucket. \n\nThere remains no major changes to the forecast, messaging and \nprojected impacts from this system specific to Southern New England. \nThe main impacts continue to be primarily over the \nsouthern/southeast waters with building rough seas and gale-force \nwinds, with some beach erosion to east-facing beach exposures. Over \nland, the main impacts continue to be breezy to gusty northeast \nwinds, in particular for southeast New England. These gusts should \nagain fall into the 20-25 mph range over the interior, but reach \ninto the 25-35 mph range for most of the coastal plain away from \nCape Cod, and could gust up to 40 mph for Nantucket. Guidance does \nshow more in the way of cloud cover for Wednesday and Thursday for \nthe southeast New England coastal plain, with light rain showers as \nwell, but there ends up being several hours of dry weather in \nbetween. Best chance for rain ends up being on Wednesday, as the \ncirculation pulls away and a reinforcing shot of cooler air as high \npressure builds back southward into Thursday. \n\nExpect plentiful sunshine away from the coasts, with seasonably mild \ndays (upper 50s to lower 60s) and cool nights (mid 30s to low 40s). \nNear the eastern and southeast coasts, cloud cover and stiff onshore \nbreezes will result in a narrower range in temperatures, with lows \nin the mid 40s to low 50s, and highs in the mid to mid/upper 50s. It \nwill feel a bit cooler with the northerly breezes though.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Prolonged stretch of dry weather Friday into\nearly next week, except for a brief risk of showers across the\neastern coastal waters Friday morning. Cooler Friday, followed \nby moderating temperatures into early next week.\n\nOnce the nor'easter departs farther offshore, a large high\npressure over eastern Canada takes over. This high pressure\nshifts south across the Northeast, reaching the central\nAppalachians by Monday. Friday looks to be the coldest day of\nthis portion of the forecast, followed by a warming trend this\nweekend into at least Monday. Some question as to how quickly a\ncold front moves past our region Monday night into Tuesday. At\npresent, Tuesday looks like we'll be on the colder side of this\nfront. However, this timing is not locked in yet.\n\nMuch of this portion of the forecast will remain dry. The only\nexception will be a chance for showers across the eastern\ncoastal waters as a weak surface trough associated with the\nnor'easter south of the Maritimes passes by. Even then, much of\nthis time will be dry across those waters.",
